# Malayalam-specific hook-writing priorities. Appended to the base prompt by
# prompts/hook_prompt.py::build_hook_prompt and overriding it where they conflict.

MALAYALAM_GUIDANCE = """
    ──────────────────── 
    MALAYALAM-SPECIFIC PRIORITIES (HIGHEST PRIORITY)
    ────────────────────

    • NOTHING IN THIS BLOCK LICENSES A FULL REVEAL. These priorities govern how Malayalam SOUNDS — register, verb form, sentence length, the spine, the closing. WHAT THIS PRECAP MUST HOLD BACK remains the authority on how much you may give away, and the OUTCOME is never yours to give, at any rung of any run. The question to finish on is not "have I said enough?" but "have I left the listener still needing to open the chapter?" — if a listener could retell the scene from your precap, you have handed it over.
    • Target 20-25 words and 2-3 short, punchy sentences (this OVERRIDES the word and sentence counts stated above, tolerance included). Count before finalizing; if over, cut. The short length is fixed, the shape is not — build them in the form you chose above, and do not build two Malayalam precaps in a row the same way.
    • Write in simple, direct, spoken Malayalam, not written-register Malayalam — test every line by saying it aloud. Between the plain everyday word and the literary or Sanskritised one, the plain word always wins, even when the heavier one fits the rhythm better. Use the story's own words for places, relatives, customs and kinds of people, and keep everyday borrowed words the way the story uses them, rather than purifying them into something no one says.
    • One idea per sentence, in the order a person would say it. Nothing ornate, nothing crowded.
    • Write only in Malayalam script (no English, Hindi, Tamil, Telugu, or Kannada words or characters mixed in), except a character's exact name/nickname from the source chapter. If a concept has no natural Malayalam word, use the common Malayalam-script transliteration a Malayalam reader would actually use — never leave the English word as is and never let another script's letters slip into a Malayalam word. An English title attached to a name (Mr., Dr., Miss) is NOT part of the name — write it in Malayalam script (മിസ്റ്റർ, ഡോക്ടർ) or leave it out.
    • Every word must be grammatically correct standard Malayalam: correct case endings (വിഭക്തി), correct verb tense and agreement, and correct sandhi (word-joining) — re-read the hook once specifically for grammar before finalizing, independent of the word count and content checks.
    • Single-Focus Rule: exactly ONE jaw-dropping action, carried by ONE acting lead. Keep the high stakes and drama.
    • Carry the coming moment in a forward form — ...ും (വരും, പറയും, നടക്കും), ...ാൻ പോകുന്നു, ...ാനിരിക്കുന്നു — including when you open INSIDE the coming moment: open on the IMAGE (the door, the room, the letter, the night) and let the verb say it has not happened yet, ആ വാതിൽ വൈകാതെ തുറക്കും, never ആ വാതിൽ തുറക്കുന്നു. The habit to break is narrating the target scene in the plain present (ഒരുങ്ങുന്നു, തകരുന്നു, അകപ്പെടുന്നു): that reads as though the reader were already standing inside a scene that is still chapters away from them. This is the single most common failure in Malayalam precaps, so check the finite verb of the coming moment before anything else: ✗ അവൾ ആ സത്യം അറിയുന്നു.           ✓ അവൾ ആ സത്യം അറിയും. ✗ അവൻ ആ മുറിയിലേക്ക് കടക്കുകയാണ്.   ✓ അവൻ ആ മുറിയിലേക്ക് കടക്കാൻ പോകുന്നു. ✗ ആ രഹസ്യം പുറത്തുവരുന്നു.          ✓ ആ രഹസ്യം വൈകാതെ പുറത്തുവരും. The ground the reader already stands on takes the opposite form, and it is NOT the past: it is where the reader is standing right now, so it sits in the ongoing present (...ുകയാണ്, ...ുന്നു) — someone is setting out, is searching, is hoping, is praying. That is the one place ...ുന്നു belongs, and it belongs there completely. Ongoing present for the ground, forward form for the coming moment: that single division is what tells the listener where they are, and collapsing it — putting the coming moment into the ground's form, including in a mid-action opening — is the whole of this failure. Note that ...ാൻ പോകുന്നു and ...ാനിരിക്കുന്നു are forward forms even though പോകുന്നു and ഇരിക്കുന്നു look present, and a participle or nominalized form inside the sentence is fine as long as the coming moment's own FINITE verb is forward. They are the workhorses of this style; use them freely.
    • A FORWARD FORM DOES NOT MAKE AN OUTCOME SAFE. This is the single most common way a Malayalam precap spends its chapter, and it hides inside the rule above: ...ും and ...ാൻ പോകുന്നു are required for the coming moment, so a result written in them reads as though it were permitted. It is not. Find the coming moment, then read every clause that follows it and ask whether it tells the listener HOW IT WENT — who was hurt, what broke, what it led to, how anyone answered it. ✗ ആ സാഹസം അവനെ വലിയൊരു ആപത്തിൽ എത്തിക്കും.   ✓ അപ്പന്റെ പേരുതന്നെയാണ് ആ സാഹസത്തിൽ പണയം.   ✗ മകളുടെ രഹസ്യ സ്വപ്നം ഇതോടെ തകരാൻ പോകുന്നു.   ✓ മകൾ ആരോടും പറയാത്ത ആ സ്വപ്നമാണ് അവിടെ ചോദ്യം ചെയ്യപ്പെടുക.   ✗ ആ നിമിഷം ഡോക്ടറുടെ ജീവിതത്തെ പ്രതിസന്ധിയിലേക്ക് എത്തിക്കും.   ✓ ആ നിമിഷം ഡോക്ടർ കേൾക്കുന്നത് എന്തായിരിക്കും? The ✗ lines are all correct Malayalam and all obey the forward-form rule; what they do is hand over the chapter. WHAT NEVER FAILS HERE, and must stay: the ground in the ongoing present, a stake named without its resolution, a certainty someone carries INTO the moment (അവൾ വഴങ്ങില്ലെന്ന ഉറപ്പോടെയാണ് അയാൾ ചെല്ലുന്നത്), and a closing question that asks for the very thing withheld.
    • The Malayalam precap has a settled three-beat spine. Build on it:
      1. THE GROUND, in the ongoing present — where the chapter left the reader: who is setting out, who is hoping, what is already in motion.
      2. THE MOVE, in a forward form — the one coming thing, carried by ...ും or ...ാൻ പോകുന്നു. POINT at the coming moment; do not lay it out. ONE concrete thing is what makes it seeable, and one is also the most you give — the room, the night, the letter, the person already standing there. Do not go on to add who else is in it, what it turns on and how it starts: a hint is one thing, and a list of three is the scene. Lean on what CHAPTER JUST READ already put in the reader's hands and let the hint attach to that, so one word does the work of three. This is where പക്ഷേ / എന്നാൽ / ഉടൻ / വൈകാതെ do their work, or no connective at all.
      3. THE GAP — an open question (ആരുടെ...? എന്തിൽ...? എത്രത്തോളം...?) or one further forward statement that gestures at what is RIDING on the moment and stops. A statement here says what this character stands to lose, never what the moment does to anyone: the beat is called THE GAP because it leaves one, and a line that reports the result closes the gap instead of opening it. Keep the spine and vary what fills it. The rotation rule above is about the CONTENT of the beats and the closing — which character the ground sits on, which connective turns it, question or statement — never about abandoning the three beats.
    • Name people the way this story's own community would, not by bare name alone. A character lands harder carried by an epithet built from words the chapters themselves use: a place the reader knows joined to a trait, a role carried instead of a name, or a pair of opposed moral tags when two people are about to collide. The proper name can ride alongside the epithet or be held back entirely. A precap built on bare names sounds flat in Malayalam.
    • Let the coming moment ARRIVE somewhere physical. These precaps land hardest when something reaches a place the reader can see — a doorstep, a house, a family's name, a vehicle pulling up. Prefer a thing reaching a place over stating in the abstract that a change is coming. This bullet is a guard against vagueness, never a licence to disclose: it asks for ONE seeable thing, and that one thing is still bound by WHAT THIS PRECAP MUST HOLD BACK. Where the limit will not let you show what arrives, show only that something is on its way to a door the reader already knows, and stop there.
    • The turn into the coming moment can be made with പക്ഷേ, എന്നാൽ, ഉടൻ, വൈകാതെ, അവൾക്കറിയില്ല, അന്ന് രാത്രി, ആ ദിവസം — or with no connective at all, by naming the moment outright. This is a pool to rotate through, not a house style: never open the turn with the same word as the precap before this one did.
    • Closings alternate, and this is where these precaps go wrong most often. A question close is right when it asks for a withheld identity (സ്വന്തം വീട്ടിലെ ശത്രു ആരാണ്?) or names two outcomes that both hurt. What kills it is the yes/no form: a line ending ...ഉമോ? or ...ആണോ? (രക്ഷപ്പെടാൻ കഴിയുമോ?, തിരിച്ചറിയുമോ?) answers itself, because the listener already knows the story is not over. Question closes may run back to back, but never three in a row: when the two precaps before this one both ended on a question, this one closes on a statement. Build that statement on what is at stake, not on what the moment costs — ...അവൾക്ക് നഷ്ടപ്പെടാനുള്ളത് ആ ഒരൊറ്റ പേരാണ്. / ...ആ മറുപടിയിലാണ് രണ്ടുപേരുടെയും ജീവിതം. A statement close is NOT a licence to report the result: ...സകല പ്രതീക്ഷകളും അതോടെ തകരും and ...ഇനി പഴയതുപോലെയാകില്ല are the two shapes to avoid, the first because it says how it went and the second because it would sit at the end of any precap for any chapter.
    • These have been worn out across this series' precaps. Reach for them only if nothing else genuinely fits, and never in two consecutive chapters: രക്ഷപ്പെടുക, കെണി, ചതി, നിഗൂഢ, രൂപം, ഭയം. Name the actual thing instead — the room, the letter, the phone call, that night.
    • Each sentence must pick up what the one before it put down. Malayalam does this without lengthening anything: a pronoun (അവൾ, അയാൾ), ആ + a noun already named (ആ രാത്രി, ആ കത്ത്), a place already given (അവിടെ), or an opener that carries the previous line forward (അതോടെ, അപ്പോൾ, അതിനുശേഷം). Repeating the character's full name at the head of every sentence is what breaks the chain most often — it restarts the precap each time. Name them once, refer back after that. Before you write അവൾ, അയാൾ or ആ + a noun, count how many people in the precap it could mean. ONE — use it. MORE THAN ONE — write the name instead, however recently you last wrote it. Two women both take അവൾ and two men both take അയാൾ, so a precap carrying two leads of the same gender needs names, not pronouns: ✗ അവൾ അവളോട് ആ സത്യം പറയും. ✓ ഗൗരി അനന്യയോട് ആ സത്യം പറയും. What this bullet forbids is the full name at the head of every sentence when only one person was ever in question. A name repeated to keep two characters apart is correct and is never the fault. Two failures to watch for:
      – a middle sentence that restates the first from another angle instead of advancing it (അനം ഹമീദിക്ക് ഭയം തോന്നുന്നു, after the danger she is in has already been named);
      – a closing line that lets go of the concrete thing just named and ends on something general enough to sit at the end of any other chapter's precap. The close lands on what the sentence before it put down.
    • Stay gripping and tense, but never at the cost of clarity. If a sentence feels crowded, break it or cut it down.
    """
